Managers and Leaders (including supervisors)
(4)
Managers and leaders, including supervisors are responsible for ensuring that:
Specialist equipment and materials that emit ionising radiation are identified within areas of responsibility and documented centrally with the Health and Safety team;
Strict security protocols are followed and consistently maintained;
Licencing requirements are clarified early, sought and obtained before any Radiation Practice commences;
Risk assessment processes are undertaken and the identified operational controls are rigorously exercised;
Commensurate training and induction is undertaken and recorded;
Health monitoring requirements are strictly followed;
Local area emergency processes are developed, exercised and periodically reviewed;
The periodic (assurance) review of control processes and licencing provisions;
Plans for the “end of life” disposal of these materials and equipment.

Health and Safety Team / Radiation Safety Officer
(5)
The Health and Safety Team/Radiation Safety Officer are responsible for:
Obtaining and managing the regulatory requirements, including licencing, for the University;
Managing the differing regulatory requirements across jurisdictions and report to regulatory bodies;
Managing the security protocols;
Providing oversight and monitor this procedure;
Providing advice on hazards and risks to consider;
Providing advice on risk control measures and emergency preparedness;
Managing and review health monitoring;
Supporting assurance processes;
Supporting incident response, investigation and share the lessons learnt across the organisation.

Direct Ionising Radiation Users
(6)
Direct Ionising Radiation Users are responsible for:
Obtaining and maintaining the relevant Use Licence ( for medical equipment, portable density gauges, servicing x-rays) when required, and ensuring requirements are in place before commencing any radiation practice;
Participating in processes to manage the risks associated with ionising radiation and ensure that the documents in use are current (risk assessment, safe work procedure, Safety Data Sheets);
Ensuring training and induction requirements are completed before any radiation practise commences;
Ensuring health monitoring devices (such as dosimeters) are worn and monitoring processes are strictly followed;
Ensuring the licencing requirements and operational controls are rigorously exercised.
